1.6.2 Single Responsibility Principle — SRP

Basic

46. What is SRP?


47. What does "one reason to change" mean?


48. What is a responsibility?


49. Why does SRP matter?


50. Give an example of a class violating SRP.



Intermediate

51. Identify the responsibilities in:



class InvoiceService {
    void calculateTotal() {}
    void saveToDatabase() {}
    void sendEmail() {}
    void generatePdf() {}
}

52. Why are these different reasons to change?


53. How would you split this class?


54. How does SRP reduce regression risk?


55. How does SRP improve testing?


56. How does SRP improve cohesion?



The source uses this exact style of example and separates calculation, persistence, email and PDF generation into focused components. 

Advanced

57. How do you identify responsibilities in a 1,000-line service?


58. Can a class have multiple methods and still obey SRP?


59. Can a class be too small to justify separation?


60. What is over-fragmentation?


61. How does business responsibility differ from technical responsibility?


62. How does cohesion help determine SRP boundaries?



Traps

63. "One class = one method" — why is this wrong?


64. "Every database operation needs its own class" — always true?


65. "Large class = SRP violation" — always true?


66. Can splitting a class actually make the architecture worse?


67. Can excessive SRP produce hundreds of meaningless classes?



The source explicitly warns against interpreting SRP as one method per class and against meaningless fragmentation. 

Design

68. Design an order-processing system following SRP.


69. Separate validation, pricing, persistence, notification and reporting.


70. Decide which responsibilities belong together.


71. Explain your class boundaries.


72. Design packages around meaningful responsibilities.



Debugging Challenges

73. Find SRP violations in a God class.


74. Identify the different reasons the class changes.


75. Refactor the class incrementally.


76. Ensure the refactoring doesn't duplicate business logic.


77. Verify that tests remain valid.



Production

78. How would you refactor a 2,000-line production service?


79. How would you avoid breaking existing clients?


80. How would you determine whether a new class boundary is meaningful?


81. When would you leave a large class alone?


82. How does SRP affect deployment and team ownership?




