1.6.3 Open/Closed Principle — OCP

Basic

83. What is OCP?


84. What does "open for extension, closed for modification" mean?


85. Why is OCP useful?


86. How does polymorphism help OCP?


87. How does composition help OCP?



Intermediate

88. Identify the OCP problem:



class PaymentProcessor {
    void process(String type) {
        if (type.equals("CARD")) {
            // ...
        } else if (type.equals("UPI")) {
            // ...
        }
    }
}

89. Why does adding a payment type require modifying this class?


90. How could an interface solve this?


91. How could the Strategy pattern help?


92. How could dependency injection help?



Advanced

93. Design an OCP-compliant payment system.


94. How do you identify genuine extension points?


95. When is a switch statement actually acceptable?


96. Can OCP cause excessive abstraction?


97. How does OCP interact with YAGNI?


98. When should OCP not be forced?



Traps

99. Does OCP mean existing code can never change?


100. Should every if/else automatically be replaced with polymorphism?


101. Should every switch become a strategy?


102. Is an interface automatically an OCP solution?


103. Can OCP create unnecessary complexity?



Design

104. Design:



PaymentProcessor
       ↓
PaymentStrategy
 ├── CardPayment
 ├── UPIPayment
 ├── WalletPayment
 └── NetBankingPayment

105. Add a new payment method.


106. Ensure existing processor code remains unchanged.


107. Explain dependency direction.


108. Explain where OCP is useful and where it isn't.



Debugging Challenges

109. Find modification hotspots.


110. Identify code repeatedly modified whenever a new type is introduced.


111. Refactor the hotspot.


112. Verify existing behavior remains unchanged.


113. Check whether the new abstraction actually reduced change impact.



Production

114. How would you design extension points in a large application?


115. How do you avoid building an unnecessary plugin framework?


116. How do you balance OCP against KISS?


117. How do you decide which variation deserves polymorphism?




