🔥 1.6 Final Integrated Challenges

After finishing individual topics, these should combine all the principles.

Challenge 1 — Payment System

Design:

Payment
 ├── Card
 ├── UPI
 ├── Wallet
 └── Net Banking

You must demonstrate:

SRP

OCP

LSP

ISP

DIP

Composition

Low coupling

High cohesion

DRY

KISS

YAGNI



---

Challenge 2 — God Class

Start with:

class OrderManager {

    void validate() {}
    void calculatePrice() {}
    void save() {}
    void sendEmail() {}
    void generateInvoice() {}
    void processPayment() {}
    void audit() {}
}

Your task:

Identify → Explain → Refactor → Test → Defend the design.


---

Challenge 3 — Legacy Inheritance

Given a deep hierarchy:

BasePayment
     ↓
OnlinePayment
     ↓
CardPayment
     ↓
CreditCardPayment
     ↓
InternationalCreditCardPayment

Determine:

LSP problems

coupling

inheritance misuse

whether composition is better

appropriate abstraction boundaries



---

Challenge 4 — Production Code Review

Given a real-world service containing:

1,500 lines

20 dependencies

multiple if/else branches

direct database creation

large interfaces

deep getter chains

duplicated business rules

speculative abstractions


Identify which principle applies to which problem, then decide which changes are actually worth making.


---

🏆 Module 1.6 Mastery Standard

The source's final mastery gate requires you to explain all five SOLID principles, implement them, identify violations, use composition, reason about coupling/cohesion, apply DRY/KISS/YAGNI appropriately, understand Law of Demeter and Tell Don't Ask, refactor major design smells, design an extensible payment system, review production code, and—most importantly—know when not to apply a principle. 

So your final learning flow for every 1.6 topic is now fixed as:

> 1. Basic
↓
2. Intermediate
↓
3. Advanced
↓
4. Traps / Common Mistakes
↓
5. Design
↓
6. Debugging Challenges
↓
7. Production



This is the same progression you used for the earlier modules, and it is a much better structure for deep Java mastery than simply having Basic/Intermediate/Advanced/Interview questions.
